-- title:
--   Theorem 14 — aggregate soundness: $\mathbb P[S\ge\tau]\le\inf_{\lambda>0}e^{-\lambda\tau}\Lambda_{\mathcal Q}(\lambda)^N$, exact $\Lambda_{\mathcal Q_\gamma}$, $\gamma=\varepsilon_0$ under gating
-- statement:
--   Consider a routed verification protocol run for $N$ rounds on a false statement, with credits $u_A,u_B>0$ and threshold $\tau\in\mathbb R$; the verifier accepts iff $S=\sum_{j\le N}u_{\rho_j}a_j\ge\tau$. For a set $\mathcal Q\subseteq[0,1]^2$ and $\lambda>0$ put
--
--   $$
--   \Lambda_{\mathcal Q}(\lambda)=1+\sup_{(q_A,q_B)\in\mathcal Q}\bigl[q_A(e^{\lambda u_A}-1)+q_B(e^{\lambda u_B}-1)\bigr],\qquad \eta_X(\lambda)=\varepsilon_X(e^{\lambda u_X}-1).
--   $$
--
--   1. If $\mathcal Q$ is a soundness profile of the run, then every prover makes the verifier accept with probability at most
--   $$
--   \mathbb P[S\ge\tau]\le\inf_{\lambda>0}e^{-\lambda\tau}\Lambda_{\mathcal Q}(\lambda)^N .
--   $$
--   2. For $\varepsilon_A,\varepsilon_B,\gamma\in[0,1]$ and $\lambda>0$, the fresh-coin profile $\mathcal Q_\gamma$ of Lemma 13 has exactly
--   $$
--   \Lambda_{\mathcal Q_\gamma}(\lambda)=1+\eta_B(\lambda)+\gamma\bigl(\eta_A(\lambda)-\eta_B(\lambda)\bigr)^+ .
--   $$
--   3. Under arm soundness (HA) with errors $\varepsilon_A,\varepsilon_B$, if $\gamma$ bounds the A-forgery rate, then
--   $$
--   \mathbb P[S\ge\tau]\le\inf_{\lambda>0}e^{-\lambda\tau}\Bigl(1+\eta_B(\lambda)+\gamma\bigl(\eta_A(\lambda)-\eta_B(\lambda)\bigr)^+\Bigr)^N .
--   $$
--   4. If the routes are the routes $\rho_j=\rho(\nu_j)$ of a signal system with the gating condition $w_1>1-\theta$ of Lemma 11, and base soundness (H0) holds with error $\varepsilon_0$, then the bound of item 3 holds with $\gamma=\varepsilon_0$.
--
--   The bound of item 3 depends on the routing only through $\gamma$, so it is the same for every signal system with the same forgery rate, including prover-chosen routes. This is the paper's main soundness result: an exponential-moment bound for arbitrary positive credits and thresholds, valid against adaptive provers.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: every prover interacting with the verifier on a false statement → every probability space $(\Omega,\mu)$, every filtration $(\mathcal F_j)$, every family of post-base-stage σ-algebras $\mathcal G_j$ and every adapted run $(\rho,a)$ (`IsRoutedRun`); reason: the run of any prover is such a run, with $\mathcal F_j$ generated by the first $j$ rounds and $\mathcal G_j$ by $\mathcal F_{j-1}$ and $\nu_j$; the theorem specializes to the paper's
--   - encoding: the hypothesis $x\notin L$ → not modelled; reason: the run is the run on a false statement; the hypotheses (H0), (HA) and the forgery bound are what $x\notin L$ supplies
--   - encoding: rounds $j=1,\dots,N$ conditioned on $\mathcal F_{j-1}$, answers $a_j\in\{0,1\}$ → 0-based rounds $j<N$ conditioned on `F j`, answers in `Bool`; reason: relabelling; same instances
--   - encoding: $\mathbb P[S\ge\tau]$ and conditional probabilities $\mathbb P[E\mid\mathcal F]$ almost surely → `μ.real {ω | τ ≤ creditSum …}` and `condProb` (Mathlib `condExp` of the indicator), with `∀ᵐ`; reason: Lean convention; the events are measurable, so these are the paper's quantities
--   - encoding: $\inf_{\lambda>0}$ and $\sup_{(q_A,q_B)\in\mathcal Q}$ → real `⨅` over `Set.Ioi 0` and real `sSup` of the image of $\mathcal Q$; reason: the family is nonnegative and $\mathcal Q\subseteq[0,1]^2$ is bounded, so both are the true infimum and supremum
--   - reading: (HA): for every view $\nu$ and prover strategy, $V_X$ accepts $\Pi_X$ on $(x,\nu)$ with probability at most $\varepsilon_X$ → $\mathbb P[a_j=1\mid\mathcal G_j]\le\varepsilon_{\rho_j}$ almost surely in every round $j<N$ (`ArmSound`); reason: p.6 states that by fresh arm coins (HA) holds for every history, and the proof of Lemma 13 uses exactly this conditional form; no model of interactive machines is formalized
--   - generalization: $\gamma=\sup\mathbb P[\rho_j=A\mid\mathcal F_{j-1}]$ over provers, rounds and histories → any real $\gamma$ with $\mathbb P[\rho_j=A\mid\mathcal F_{j-1}]\le\gamma$ almost surely for $j<N$ (`ForgeryBound`); reason: the paper's $\gamma$ is such a bound for every prover's run
--   - reading: $\mathcal Q_\gamma$ printed without the constraint $(q_A,q_B)\in[0,1]^2$ → $\mathcal Q_\gamma\cap[0,1]^2$ (`Qgamma`); reason: Definition 12 requires a soundness profile to be a subset of $[0,1]^2$; the intersection does not change the supremum in Theorem 14
--   - generalization: soundness errors $\varepsilon_A,\varepsilon_B,\varepsilon_0\in[0,1]$ (probabilities) in items 3 and 4 → $\varepsilon_A,\varepsilon_B\ge0$ and $\varepsilon_0$ any real; reason: the bounds hold without the upper bounds; they specialize to the paper's
--   - encoding: soundness errors $\varepsilon_A,\varepsilon_B$ and forgery rate $\gamma$, which are probabilities (§2, p.4; "trivially $\gamma\le1$", p.7) → explicit hypotheses $\varepsilon_A,\varepsilon_B,\gamma\in[0,1]$ in item 2 (the exact supremum); reason: the source's quantities always satisfy them; they make the corners $(g\varepsilon_A,(1-g)\varepsilon_B)$, $g\in[0,\gamma]$, lie in $[0,1]^2$, so the intersection with $[0,1]^2$ does not change the supremum
--   - generalization: efficiently computable signals $\sigma_i:\{0,1\}^*\to[0,1]$ → maps $\sigma_i:W\to[0,1]$ on an arbitrary type $W$ of views; reason: the paper uses only that each $\sigma_i$ maps into $[0,1]$ (p.5); bit-string views are an instance
--   - encoding: signals $\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_k$ with weights $w_1,\dots,w_k$, correctness signal $\sigma_1$ with $\sigma_1(\nu)=1[V_0\text{ accepts in }\nu]$ → signals indexed by `Fin (k+1)`, index `0` the correctness signal, with $\sigma_1=\mathbf 1_{\mathrm{acc}_0}$ for the set $\mathrm{acc}_0$ of accepting views (`IsCorrectnessSignal`); reason: relabelling of the printed convention; $V_0$'s acceptance is the set $\mathrm{acc}_0$
--   - encoding: the base view $\nu_j$ produced in step (R1), routed by $\rho_j=\rho(\nu_j)$ → $\nu_j$ is $\mathcal G_j$-measurable into a measurable space $W$, $\mathrm{acc}_0$ is measurable, and $\rho_j=\rho\circ\nu_j$; reason: $\mathcal G_j$ is the information after the base stage, which contains $\nu_j$; on bit strings every set is measurable
--   - encoding: "the bound is the same for every signal system with the same $\gamma$" → carried by the statement shape: clauses (i)–(iii) hold for arbitrary adapted routes and their bounds depend on the routes only through $\gamma$; reason: the sentence has no separate formal content
-- source:
--   Dumlu, Signal-Routed Two-Arm Verification, STOC 2027 submission manuscript (held version, sha256 d56b3b7a…), Theorem 14, p.7

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SigRoute_IsProfile
import Definitions.Def_SigRoute_Qgamma
import Definitions.Def_SigRoute_ArmSound
import Definitions.Def_SigRoute_SignalSystem

namespace SigRoute

open MeasureTheory

/-- Theorem 14 (Aggregate soundness) (Dumlu, Signal-Routed Two-Arm Verification, STOC 2027 submission manuscript (held version, sha256 d56b3b7a…), Theorem 14, p.7). Let `Q` be a soundness profile of RV.
For `λ > 0` put `Λ_Q(λ) = 1 + sup_{(q_A,q_B)∈Q} [q_A(e^{λu_A} − 1) + q_B(e^{λu_B} − 1)]`. Then every prover
makes the verifier accept with probability at most `inf_{λ>0} e^{−λτ} Λ_Q(λ)^N`. In particular, under (HA),
with `Q = Q_γ` and `η_X(λ) = ε_X(e^{λu_X} − 1)`, `Λ_{Q_γ}(λ) = 1 + η_B(λ) + γ(η_A(λ) − η_B(λ))⁺`. The bound
is the same for every signal system with the same `γ`, and with the gating condition of Lemma 11 one may
take `γ = ε_0`.

The four conjuncts: (i) the bound for every profile `Q`; (ii) the exact supremum on `Q_γ`; (iii) under
(HA) and a forgery bound `γ`, the bound with `1 + η_B + γ(η_A − η_B)⁺`; (iv) for a run routed by a signal
system with the gating condition `w_1 > 1 − θ`, under (H0) and (HA), the bound with `γ = ε_0`.

**Formalization Note** The paper's "every prover" is "every probability space `(Ω, μ)`, filtration `F`, post-base-stage σ-algebras `G` and adapted run `(ρ, a)`" (`IsRoutedRun`); rounds are 0-based, so the paper's `F_{j−1}` is `F j`. (HA) is its conditional form given the post-base-stage σ-algebra
`G j` (`ArmSound`); `γ` is any almost-sure bound on `P[ρ_j = A | F_{j−1}]` (`ForgeryBound`); `Q_γ` is
intersected with `[0,1]²` (`Qgamma`). Clause (ii) assumes `ε_X, γ ∈ [0,1]`; clauses (iii) and (iv)
assume only `ε_X ≥ 0`. The routes in (i)–(iii) are arbitrary adapted routes (any signal system, or a
prover-chosen route, Remark 7), which carries "the same for every signal system with the same `γ`". The
infimum is the real `⨅` over `λ ∈ (0, ∞)` of a nonnegative family. -/
theorem theorem_14 {Ω : Type*} {m : MeasurableSpace Ω} (μ : Measure Ω)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (F : Filtration ℕ m) (G : ℕ → MeasurableSpace Ω)
    (ρ : ℕ → Ω → Arm) (a : ℕ → Ω → Bool) (hrun : IsRoutedRun F G ρ a)
    (u : Arm → ℝ) (hu : ∀ X, 0 < u X) (N : ℕ) (τ : ℝ)
    {W : Type*} [MeasurableSpace W] :
    -- (i) every soundness profile
    (∀ Q : Set (ℝ × ℝ), IsProfile μ F ρ a N Q →
      μ.real {ω | τ ≤ creditSum u ρ a N ω}
        ≤ ⨅ lam : Set.Ioi (0 : ℝ), Real.exp (-(lam : ℝ) * τ) * Lam u Q lam ^ N) ∧
    -- (ii) the exact supremum on Q_γ
    (∀ (ε : Arm → ℝ) (γ : ℝ), (∀ X, ε X ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1) → γ ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1 →
      ∀ lam : ℝ, 0 < lam →
        Lam u (Qgamma ε γ) lam
          = 1 + eta ε u lam Arm.B + γ * max (eta ε u lam Arm.A - eta ε u lam Arm.B) 0) ∧
    -- (iii) under (HA) with forgery bound γ
    (∀ (ε : Arm → ℝ) (γ : ℝ), (∀ X, 0 ≤ ε X) → ArmSound μ G ρ a N ε → ForgeryBound μ F ρ N γ →
      μ.real {ω | τ ≤ creditSum u ρ a N ω}
        ≤ ⨅ lam : Set.Ioi (0 : ℝ), Real.exp (-(lam : ℝ) * τ) *
            (1 + eta ε u lam Arm.B + γ * max (eta ε u lam Arm.A - eta ε u lam Arm.B) 0) ^ N) ∧
    -- (iv) gating: γ = ε₀
    (∀ (k : ℕ) (S : SignalSystem W k) (acc₀ : Set W), MeasurableSet acc₀ →
      S.IsCorrectnessSignal acc₀ →
      ∀ ν : ℕ → Ω → W, (∀ j, Measurable[G j] (ν j)) → (∀ j, ρ j = fun ω => S.route (ν j ω)) →
      1 - S.θ < S.w 0 →
      ∀ (ε₀ : ℝ) (ε : Arm → ℝ),
        (∀ j < N, ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ, condProb μ (F j) {ω | S.sig (ν j ω) 0 = 1} ω ≤ ε₀) →
        (∀ X, 0 ≤ ε X) → ArmSound μ G ρ a N ε →
        μ.real {ω | τ ≤ creditSum u ρ a N ω}
          ≤ ⨅ lam : Set.Ioi (0 : ℝ), Real.exp (-(lam : ℝ) * τ) *
              (1 + eta ε u lam Arm.B + ε₀ * max (eta ε u lam Arm.A - eta ε u lam Arm.B) 0) ^ N) := by sorry

end SigRoute
